        <!-- SECTION 1: What Is Polymeric Sand -->
        <div id="what-is-polymeric-sand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Is Polymeric Sand and Why Does It Matter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Polymeric sand is a fine aggregate mixed with polymer binders that activate when exposed to water. When properly applied to paver joints and misted with water, the binders cure into a firm, semi-rigid compound that locks joint sand in place, resists weed germination, deters ant tunneling, and withstands rain and irrigation washout.
          </p>

          <p>
            Regular jointing sand stays loose permanently — it washes out in heavy rain, blows out in wind, and provides a perfect germination bed for weeds. Within two to three seasons, unprotected paver joints filled with regular sand are typically full of grass, moss, or creeping weeds, and the pavers themselves begin to shift as the joint stability disappears. <strong>Polymeric sand eliminates this maintenance cycle.</strong>
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Polymeric Sand vs. Regular Sand</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">5-10 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Polymeric sand lifespan</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1-2 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Regular sand before washout</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Zero</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Weed germination in hardened joints</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">$2-4/bag</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Additional cost vs. regular sand</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 2: How to Choose -->
        <div id="how-to-choose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How to Choose the Right Polymeric Sand for Your Pavers
          </h2>

          <p>
            The right polymeric sand depends on four variables: <strong>joint width</strong>, <strong>paver type and color</strong>, <strong>traffic load</strong>, and <strong>climate</strong>. Getting this selection wrong is the primary reason polymeric sand installations fail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Joint Width</h3>
          <p>
            Most standard polymeric sand products work in joints from 1/8 inch to 3/4 inch — the range typical of concrete interlocking pavers. Natural stone pavers, irregular flagstone, and tumbled concrete pavers often have wider gaps (up to 1.5 inches). Using a product outside its rated joint width range results in incomplete hardening in wide joints and excessive surface binder in narrow joints. Always check the joint width rating before purchasing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Paver Color and Texture</h3>
          <p>
            Light-colored or textured pavers are the highest-risk application for hazing — the milky white surface contamination caused by polymer binder migration during activation. On dark pavers, hazing may be invisible or removable. On buff, tan, or cream pavers, hazing can be permanent. For any light-colored or expensive paver installation, specify a no-haze formula like DOMINATOR or Techniseal HP, and follow the activation protocol precisely.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Traffic Load</h3>
          <p>
