        <div id="application-guide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Apply Polymeric Sand: Step-by-Step
          </h2>

          <p>
            Application technique matters as much as product selection. A premium product incorrectly applied will fail just as quickly as a bargain product. Here's the correct process:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Ensure Pavers Are Bone Dry</h3>
          <p>
            This is non-negotiable. Polymeric sand applied to damp pavers will haze — guaranteed. Wait at least 24-48 hours after any rain before starting. If in doubt, check the back of the paver rather than the surface; moisture hides on the underside longer than on top. On a sunny day in dry weather, the surface should feel warm to the touch with no visible moisture.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Sweep Sand Into Joints</h3>
          <p>
            Pour polymeric sand onto the paver surface and work it into joints with a push broom, sweeping in multiple directions. The goal is to fill every joint completely. Don't try to precisely control how much goes on the surface — you'll remove excess later.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Compact with a Plate Compactor</h3>
          <p>
            Run a plate compactor (with a rubber mat to protect the paver surface) over the entire installation. Compaction drives sand deeper into joints and reveals where additional fill is needed. Sweep more sand into any voids, then compact again. The joint should be filled to within 1/8 inch of the paver surface — not flush, not overfull.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Remove ALL Excess Sand</h3>
          <p>
            This step determines whether you haze or not. Use a leaf blower on its lowest setting to remove every grain of sand from the paver surface. Do not use a broom — brooms drag sand back across the surface and embed it in texture. The paver surface should be completely clean before any water touches it. If you can see sand on the paver surface, keep blowing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Activate with Water</h3>
          <p>
            Use a garden hose with a gentle spray nozzle — never a pressure washer or jet spray. Follow the three-stage misting protocol: gentle mist for 30 seconds, wait 3 minutes, gentle mist for 30 seconds, wait 3 minutes, final gentle mist for 30 seconds. The goal is to saturate the joints without washing sand out or splashing binder onto the paver surface. Keep off the installation for 24 hours.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Weather Window Required</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              You need at least <strong>24 hours of dry weather after activation</strong> for polymeric sand to set properly. Rain on freshly activated polymeric sand washes the polymer binders out of the joints before they cure. Check a 48-hour forecast before starting — if rain is coming within 24 hours of your planned completion time, wait for a better window.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Common Polymeric Sand Mistakes That Ruin Paver Installations
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying to Damp Pavers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The single most common cause of hazing. Moisture in the paver activates the polymer binders prematurely before the excess surface sand is removed, fusing the binders to the paver face. This manifests as a milky white residue that ranges from ugly to impossible to remove. On natural stone and textured concrete pavers, it can be permanent. Never apply polymeric sand unless you are certain the pavers have been dry for at least 24 hours.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Plate Compactor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hand-tamping or foot pressure does not achieve the compaction density that a plate compactor delivers. Without proper compaction, joints are not fully filled, and the hardened sand has air voids that allow water infiltration and joint erosion. Plate compactors can be rented for $50-75 per day — this is not a step to skip on any installation larger than 50 square feet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Pressure Washer for Activation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                High-pressure water blasts the sand out of joints before it can cure. The activation process requires a gentle mist that saturates the joint from above without displacing the sand. A standard garden hose with a fan-spray nozzle on its gentlest setting is correct. Pressure washers, jet nozzles, and even strong hose streams are all capable of destroying a freshly swept polymeric sand installation in seconds.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the Wrong Product for the Joint Width</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Standard polymeric sand in joints wider than its rated maximum results in incomplete hardening in the center of the joint. The polymer binders only penetrate and cure effectively to a certain depth and width. Wide natural stone or flagstone installations with joints over 3/4 inch require a wide-joint polymeric sand specifically formulated for larger gaps. Using a standard product produces joints that are hard at the edges but soft and erosion-prone in the center.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best polymeric sand for pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Techniseal HP Jointing Sand is our overall top pick for patio, walkway, and standard concrete paver installations. For driveways with vehicle traffic, Unilock Enduro Sand is the correct specification. For first-time DIY installations, Alliance Gator's forgiving activation process reduces the risk of beginner mistakes. For light-colored or expensive pavers where hazing is unacceptable, DOMINATOR's no-haze formula provides the best protection.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does polymeric sand last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Properly applied polymeric sand on a pedestrian paver installation lasts 5-10 years before joint top-up or replacement is needed. Vehicular-rated products on driveway installations are rated for 10+ years. Epoxy-fortified products can exceed 15 years. Poor compaction, incorrect product selection, or activation errors will dramatically shorten joint life regardless of product quality.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use polymeric sand with any type of paver?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most polymeric sand products work with concrete interlocking pavers, clay brick pavers, natural stone, and flagstone. The key variable is joint width — verify that your joint width falls within the product's rated range. Some products are specifically not recommended for very porous natural stone (certain sandstones, for example) where the binders can penetrate and stain the paver face. When in doubt, test the product on a hidden area first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What causes polymeric sand hazing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hazing is caused by polymer binders migrating onto the paver surface during water activation — almost always because the pavers were damp before application, there was residual sand on the surface when water was applied, or too much water pressure was used during activation. Prevention is straightforward: ensure pavers are bone dry, remove ALL surface sand with a leaf blower before any water contact, and use only a gentle mist spray for activation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I apply polymeric sand over existing polymeric sand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for joint top-up maintenance. If existing joints are partially eroded but the remaining hardened sand is intact, you can add fresh polymeric sand to the voids and re-activate. The new product bonds to the existing hardened base. If joints have fully failed (soft, washed out, or heavily weeded), the existing material should be removed before re-sanding. Use a joint scraper or oscillating tool to remove old material, then re-sweep, compact, and activate fresh polymeric sand.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does polymeric sand prevent weeds completely?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Properly hardened polymeric sand eliminates weed germination within the joints — the hardened compound does not provide a germination medium. However, airborne weed seeds can germinate in organic debris (leaves, dirt) that accumulates on the surface of paver joints over time. Regular sweeping to remove surface debris prevents this secondary weed source. Weeds growing through hardened polymeric sand from underneath (through the base layer) indicate base layer contamination, not a polymeric sand failure.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

