          <p>
            Always buy 10–15% more than your calculation suggests. Polymeric sand is consumed during the compaction process as it settles deeper into joints, and you'll want extra on hand for a final top-up pass after the first compaction cycle. Leftover bags store indefinitely in a dry location — but running short mid-project means stopping work with open joints exposed to weather.
          </p>

          <p>
            For natural stone or flagstone with wider joints (3/4 inch to 1.5 inches), sand consumption increases significantly — budget approximately double the quantities above. The larger void volume in wide joints requires substantially more material to fill to the correct depth of 1/8 inch below the paver surface.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Polymeric Sand and Sealing -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Should You Seal Pavers After Applying Polymeric Sand?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Polymeric sand and paver sealer are complementary products — they address different aspects of paver protection and work better together than either does alone. Polymeric sand stabilizes the joints and prevents weed germination and ant tunneling. Paver sealer protects the paver surface itself from staining, UV fading, and water absorption, while simultaneously locking the surface layer of polymeric sand into the joint.
          </p>

          <p>
            The correct sequence is always: <strong>install polymeric sand first, allow it to fully cure (minimum 24-48 hours, ideally 72 hours), then apply paver sealer over the entire surface</strong>. Applying sealer before polymeric sand cures traps moisture in the joints and can prevent the polymer binders from achieving full hardness. Applying sealer before sand is installed is an obvious sequencing error that wastes sealer and requires removal before sanding.
          </p>

          <p>
            For paver driveways specifically, applying a penetrating or film-forming paver sealer after polymeric sand installation provides an additional layer of protection against the fuel, oil, and deicing salt exposure that vehicular surfaces face. The combination of vehicular-rated polymeric sand (like Unilock Enduro Sand) and a quality paver sealer is the correct full specification for any paved driveway intended to last a decade or more with minimal maintenance.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 rounded-r-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-blue-800 mb-2">Timing: The Rule of Thumb</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-blue-700">
              Wait at least <strong>72 hours after polymeric sand activation</strong> before applying any paver sealer. Some manufacturers recommend waiting up to 30 days for the polymeric sand to achieve maximum hardness before sealing. In practice, 72 hours is sufficient for most residential applications in warm, dry conditions. In cooler or humid weather, extend this waiting period to 5–7 days.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            One final note on product compatibility: not every paver sealer works equally well over every polymeric sand formula. Water-based acrylic paver sealers are universally compatible with all polymeric sand products. Solvent-based sealers can sometimes soften or re-activate the polymer binders in the joint — check the manufacturer's compatibility documentation before specifying a solvent-based sealer over a freshly installed polymeric sand joint. When in doubt, test a small inconspicuous area first and examine the joint after the sealer fully cures to confirm no softening or visible discoloration has occurred before proceeding with the full surface installation.
          </p>
        </div>

